          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Apart from medication, what helps with long-term insomnia?",
          "answer": "Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is the main non-medication approach. The guidance in \"Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment\" says sleep medicine and internal medicine guidance both recommend it as first-line for chronic insomnia in adults, ahead of medication. The document says its benefits last after treatment ends, which medication effects generally do not. It is usually delivered over about four to eight sessions. Two of its components are:\n\n- **Stimulus control:** using the bed only for sleep, and getting up when you cannot sleep, so the bed stops signaling wakefulness.\n- **Sleep restriction:** limiting time in bed toward your actual sleep time, then gradually extending it as sleep improves.\n\nThe same document notes that alcohol shortens the time it takes to fall asleep but then fragments the second half of the night. It also says caffeine, nicotine, and shift patterns are worth reviewing.\n\nThe \"Insomnia\" reference summary adds that chronic insomnia can affect daytime function and mood, and that non-drug treatment often works well enough to avoid long-term sleeping pills. It also says that sleep hygiene advice alone is not effective treatment. I could not see the full detail of that point in the retrieved text, so I can't tell you more about it here.\n\nThe same summary lists signs that warrant assessment beyond insomnia treatment. These include loud snoring with witnessed pauses in breathing, excessive daytime sleepiness, falling asleep while driving, acting out dreams, and sleep problems alongside low mood or hopelessness. If you notice any of these, raise them with a clinician.\n\nThe search also returned passages on thyroid testing, diabetes, and reflux. They did not relate to your question, so I have not used them. The retrieved material does not cover specific CBT-I providers, how to access therapy, or which lifestyle changes beyond these are effective. A doctor or sleep specialist can help you find a CBT-I program.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
